fun getNode1(): ObjectRef<EmptyNode2D> {
    return mNode.clone()
}

// You can return a local ObjectRef directly, but do not close it explicitly.
fun getNode2(): ObjectRef<EmptyNode2D> {
    var localRef = EmptyNode2D.create(domain, "My Empty Node")

    return localRef;

    // Here, do not close the localRef.
}

```
Copy to clipboard
  * When passing an `ObjectRef[](https://docs.kanzi.com/4.1.0/en/reference/kanzi-java-api/a02659.html)` instance as an argument to a method, the ownership of that instance is retained with the caller. When implementing a method that takes an `ObjectRef[](https://docs.kanzi.com/4.1.0/en/reference/kanzi-java-api/a02659.html)` as an argument, the ownership is temporary. If the method stores the instance in a member variable, or otherwise wants to extend the lifetime of the object beyond the scope of the method, it must clone the `ObjectRef[](https://docs.kanzi.com/4.1.0/en/reference/kanzi-java-api/a02659.html)` instance.
