    2. In the cpp file of your Kanzi Engine plugin project, define the custom property types. In each property type definition, set these parameters:
      1. The fully qualified name of the property type for Kanzi Studio.
      2. The default value of the property type. See [Default property value](https://docs.kanzi.com/4.1.0/en/kanzi-fundamentals/property-system.html#default-property-value) and [Property precedence](https://docs.kanzi.com/4.1.0/en/kanzi-fundamentals/property-system.html#property-precedence).
      3. The property type change flags that indicate the effects of changing the value of the property. See [Property type change flags](https://docs.kanzi.com/4.1.0/en/kanzi-fundamentals/property-system.html#property-type-change-flags) and `PropertyTypeChangeFlag[](https://docs.kanzi.com/4.1.0/en/reference/kanzi-runtime-api/a11084.html#gae575b44cdca9c6f73824d09d5671fab2)`.
To not use any change flags, set the argument to `0`.
      4. Whether the value of the property type is inheritable. See [Inheritable property types](https://docs.kanzi.com/4.1.0/en/kanzi-fundamentals/property-system.html#inheritable-property-types).
      5. The metadata about the property type for Kanzi Studio. See [Reference for showing Kanzi Engine plugin custom types in Kanzi Studio](https://docs.kanzi.com/4.1.0/en/working-with/plugins/reference-for-showing-kanzi-engine-plugin-custom-types.html).
For example, to define the custom property types that you created in the previous step:
