Kanzi Studio plugins extend the functionality of Kanzi Studio and run in Kanzi Studio.
For example, you can extend the functionality of Kanzi Studio to:
  * Automate tasks that take a lot of time to do manually in Kanzi Studio, or are prone to errors.
  * Import and export content from Kanzi Studio, such as localization tables in a format that Kanzi Studio does not support
  * Create editors that abstract the details and speed up the workflow, such as visualization tools that support work on your projects in Kanzi Studio.


You can present a Kanzi Studio plugin either:
  * In a Kanzi Studio window that contains the plugin user interface.
  * As commands that users can execute from the Kanzi Studio main menu or from a context menu, running the plugin without a user interface.


This topic covers how to create Kanzi Studio command plugins. To learn how to create Kanzi Studio window plugins, see [Creating Kanzi Studio window plugins](https://docs.kanzi.com/4.1.0/en/working-with/plugins/creating-kanzi-studio-window-plugins.html).
The Kanzi Studio plugin API is provided as a .NET framework assembly. You can find it in `<KanziInstallation>/Studio/Bin/PluginInterface.dll`. The Kanzi Studio plugin API provides access to data and commands in Kanzi Studio. When you want to use a Kanzi Studio plugin with different versions of Kanzi Studio, build the plugin with the `PluginInterface.dll` from each version of Kanzi Studio where you want to use that plugin.
To develop a Kanzi Studio command plugin:
  1. Create the base for a Kanzi Studio plugin. See [Creating the base for a Kanzi Studio command plugin](https://docs.kanzi.com/4.1.0/en/working-with/plugins/creating-kanzi-studio-plugins.html#creating-the-base-for-command-plugin).
  2. Build and run your Kanzi Studio plugin. See [Building and running a Kanzi Studio plugin](https://docs.kanzi.com/4.1.0/en/working-with/plugins/creating-kanzi-studio-plugins.html#building-and-running-kanzi-studio-command-plugin).
  3. Add functionality to your Kanzi Studio plugin. See [Adding functionality to your Kanzi Studio command plugin](https://docs.kanzi.com/4.1.0/en/working-with/plugins/creating-kanzi-studio-plugins.html#adding-functionality-to-your-kanzi-studio-command-plugin) and [Add multiple commands to your Kanzi Studio command plugin](https://docs.kanzi.com/4.1.0/en/working-with/plugins/creating-kanzi-studio-plugins.html#add-multiple-commands-to-your-kanzi-studio-command-plugin).
  4. Debug your Kanzi Studio plugin. See [Debugging a Kanzi Studio plugin](https://docs.kanzi.com/4.1.0/en/working-with/plugins/debugging-kanzi-studio-plugins.html#debuggin-kanzi-studio-plugin).


To find detailed information about the Kanzi Studio plugin API see:
  * [Overview of Kanzi Studio plugin API](https://docs.kanzi.com/4.1.0/en/working-with/plugins/reference-kanzi-studio-plugin-interface.html)
  * [Kanzi Studio plugin API reference](https://docs.kanzi.com/4.1.0/en/reference/kanzi-studio-plugin-api/index.html)


## Creating the base for a Kanzi Studio command plugin[¶](https://docs.kanzi.com/4.1.0/en/working-with/plugins/creating-kanzi-studio-plugins.html#creating-the-base-for-a-ks-command-plugin "Link to this heading")
Kanzi Studio command plugins are plugins that you execute as commands and do not have a user interface. For example, use the command plugins to execute a command on the entire or the selected part of the node tree in your project.
Here you create the base for a Kanzi Studio command plugin where you then add functionality that extends the functionality of Kanzi Studio.
To create a Kanzi Studio command plugin:
  1. In Visual Studio select File > New > Project and create a Visual C# Class Library.
![../../_images/create-visual-c-class-library.png](https://docs.kanzi.com/4.1.0/en/_images/create-visual-c-class-library.png)
  2. In the Solution Explorer right-click the project name, select Add > Reference, and add references to files:
    1. Select Assemblies > Framework targeting .NET Framework and add the reference to: `System.ComponentModel.Composition`.
![../../_images/system-componentmodel-composition.png](https://docs.kanzi.com/4.1.0/en/_images/system-componentmodel-composition.png)
    2. Click Browse… and add the reference to the `<KanziInstallation>/Studio/Bin/PluginInterface.dll` file.
