Kanzi Studio plugins extend the functionality of Kanzi Studio and run in Kanzi Studio.
For example, you can extend the functionality of Kanzi Studio to:
  * Automate tasks that take a lot of time to do manually in Kanzi Studio, or are prone to errors.
  * Import and export content from Kanzi Studio, such as localization tables in a format that Kanzi Studio does not support
  * Create editors that abstract the details and speed up the workflow, such as visualization tools that support work on your projects in Kanzi Studio.


You can present a Kanzi Studio plugin either:
  * In a Kanzi Studio window that contains the plugin user interface.
  * As commands that users can execute from the Kanzi Studio main menu or from a context menu, running the plugin without a user interface.


This topic covers how to create Kanzi Studio window plugins. To learn how to create Kanzi Studio command plugins, see [Creating Kanzi Studio command plugins](https://docs.kanzi.com/4.1.0/en/working-with/plugins/creating-kanzi-studio-plugins.html).
The default UI framework for creating Kanzi Studio window plugins is WPF. However, you can use frameworks that run on HTML using the web browser control in WPF, or other frameworks by embedding the content using WPF HwndHost control that hosts a Win32 window as an element in WPF content.
The Kanzi Studio plugin API is provided as a .NET framework assembly. You can find it in `<KanziInstallation>/Studio/Bin/PluginInterface.dll`. The Kanzi Studio plugin API provides access to data and commands in Kanzi Studio. When you want to use a Kanzi Studio plugin with different versions of Kanzi Studio, build the plugin with the `PluginInterface.dll` from each version of Kanzi Studio where you want to use that plugin.
To develop a Kanzi Studio window plugin:
  1. Create the base for a Kanzi Studio window plugin. See [Creating the base for a Kanzi Studio window plugin](https://docs.kanzi.com/4.1.0/en/working-with/plugins/creating-kanzi-studio-window-plugins.html#creating-the-base-for-window-plugin).
  2. Build and run your Kanzi Studio plugin. See [Building and running a Kanzi Studio plugin](https://docs.kanzi.com/4.1.0/en/working-with/plugins/creating-kanzi-studio-window-plugins.html#building-and-running-kanzi-studio-plugin).
  3. Add functionality to your Kanzi Studio plugin. See [Adding functionality to your Kanzi Studio window plugin](https://docs.kanzi.com/4.1.0/en/working-with/plugins/creating-kanzi-studio-window-plugins.html#adding-functionality-to-your-kanzi-studio-window-plugin).
  4. Group project changes in your Kanzi Studio window plugin for undo and redo. See [Grouping project changes in your Kanzi Studio window plugin](https://docs.kanzi.com/4.1.0/en/working-with/plugins/creating-kanzi-studio-window-plugins.html#grouping-project-changes-in-your-kanzi-studio-window-plugin).
  5. Debug your Kanzi Studio plugin. See [Debugging a Kanzi Studio plugin](https://docs.kanzi.com/4.1.0/en/working-with/plugins/debugging-kanzi-studio-plugins.html#debuggin-kanzi-studio-plugin).


To find detailed information about the Kanzi Studio plugin API see:
  * [Overview of Kanzi Studio plugin API](https://docs.kanzi.com/4.1.0/en/working-with/plugins/reference-kanzi-studio-plugin-interface.html)
  * [Kanzi Studio plugin API reference](https://docs.kanzi.com/4.1.0/en/reference/kanzi-studio-plugin-api/index.html)


